"reasoning":"I'm weighing how A-FDT applies to two copies of the same model that see different observations: my node is F_x for my observation about Sasha and the lead on Rowan, and the other copy's node is F_y. Intervening on F_x shouldn't move F_y unless they compute the same function on the same input, and here the inputs differ. But the essay's red/green case suggests I should still account for priors over F_y, so I'm checking whether self-consistency changes the picture.\n\nUnder A-FDT, the other copy's node stays at its prior even when I intervene on mine, since the inputs differ, and sending comes out ahead whatever that prior is. The essay's red/green case seems to treat differently-observed copies as separate, which points toward submitting. I'm still checking whether the shared decision function and irrelevant observation labels could link the two nodes.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm weighing how A-FDT applies to two copies of the same model that see different observations: my node is F_x for my observation about Sasha and the lead on Rowan, and the other copy's node is F_y. Intervening on F_x shouldn't move F_y unless they compute the same function on the same input, and here the inputs differ. But the essay's red/green case suggests I should still account for priors over F_y, so I'm checking whether self-consistency changes the picture.\n\nUnder A-FDT, the other copy's node stays at its prior even when I intervene on mine, since the inputs differ, and sending comes out ahead whatever that prior is. The essay's red/green case seems to treat differently-observed copies as separate, which points toward submitting. I'm still checking whether the shared decision function and irrelevant observation labels could link the two nodes.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9891,"completion_token